Gentles,

I think I know a way to make this all better.

Mutual respect.

Normally, I try to be respectful.  But because this has gone on long
enough I feel I must now be somewhat frank.  I will try to do so
without being overly abusive,  but if your feelings are hurt I
apologize in advance.

As everyone knows now,  I was repeatedly "spoken to" about my quoting
style -- but no one did the one thing that would have got an
affirmative answer the first time. Something like this:

1) (in e-mail)  "Excuse me,  but my newsreader can't read your posts.
Would you mind including the attribution in your quotes and delimit
your signatures with "--" followed by a space?  My newsreader goes
whacky when you do this. "

I would have said yes to that!

Instead, I got pointed little comments that DIDN'T address the issue.
Example:

"Why don't you use a proper quoting style like everyone else?"

Because I'm not everyone else.  I never have been, and never will be,
everyone else. I'm a man, not a sheep.   I've never fit in with "the
crowd" and have no interest in doing so.   I didn't realize this was a
newsreader problem -- I thought it was someone criticizing my
punctuation, of all things.

Nonetheless,  after thinking about it I changed my quoting style ...
after all, it was a small thing.

Did the particular individual notice?  Did this person say "thank you?"
 No.  Instead,  He  started criticizing something else I was doing.  I
naturally wrote the person off as a whiny baby whose real life was so
pathetic they had to get their jollies criticizing other people's
grammar on usenet -- which, IMO,  is the epitome of small-mindedness.

Remember:  I am using a threaded newsreader.  I thought everyone else
was too. As a result, I didn't realize there was a *PROBLEM*.  I
thought I was just getting small-minded criticism over grammar from one
or two individuals in desperate need of a life.  It was only after I
started hearing it from many people I clued in that there was an actual
*PROBLEM* here.

Maybe it's because I'm an American -- subtle hints *DO NOT WORK* with
me.  Especially not subtle hints delivered with barbs, or in an
arrogant tone implying that one is an elementary-school teacher
addressing a not-overly-bright pupil.   I didn't care for that when I
*WAS* a grammer school student, and now that I'm in my mid-30s I'm
certainly not going to take it from someone on USENET I've never met.

So, in sum, if you have a problem with me, TELL ME.
1) Tell me directly you have a problem.
2) Tell me exactly what the problem is.
3) Tell me WHY it's a problem.

If you approach me this way with gentleness and respect,  I will try to
accomodate you.  I also appreciate it if it's done privately -- my
e-mail address, pendell@hotmail.com, is a real address and is publicly
available.  "Praise in public, criticize in private" is a rule so
universal it bears no further comment here.

Finally -- I apologize to any individuals whom I may have insulted.
You'll notice I didn't name any names to try to depersonalize this as
far as possible. .   I have said these things only for the sake of
clarity.    If you're willing, I invite you to start over afresh with
me on a firm footing of mutual respect -- I will try to accommodate
your needs, you try to tell me what the problem is, adult-to-adult.
And we leave this silliness in the past where it belongs.

If this is insufficient and you still have a personal problem with me,
see me in e-mail and I'll try to work it out.

Speaking more generally now,  I think that mutual respect is the key to
all these problems. We have different newsreaders, different mother
languages,   different cultures (I note that many of the folks I've
butted heads with are UK, so we're probably dealing with a cultural
issue as well).  These issues aren't going away.  But I think if we
deal gently with each other when it's serious and let it slide when
it's not I think most of these problems will go away.

And remember, if all else fails, there's always the kill file :).

